<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prokhorovka culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prokhorovka culture is the early Sarmatian archaeological culture of the steppes of the southern Urals and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, dated to about the fourth to second centuries BCE. Named after kurgans excavated near Prokhorovka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a> region, it is known from burials with long swords, arrowheads and horse gear, a notable share of them of armed women. It marks the westward expansion of the Sarmatians towards the Don.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prokhorovka-culture.jpg" alt="Prokhorovka culture" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Conrad Cichorius (1863–1932) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarmatian_cataphract_in_Trajan%27s_colmun,_2nd_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
